<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hallein</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hallein is a town on the Salzach in the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, south of the city of Salzburg. Its salt mine on the Dürrnberg, already worked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, was the main source of the prince-archbishops' wealth. Mining continued until 1989, and the Celtic finds are shown in the town's museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hallein.jpg" alt="Hallein" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hallein_vom_Barmstoa2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hallein is the capital of Hallein district in the Tennengau region. The town stretches along the Salzach river in the shadow of the Untersberg massif, close to the German border on the west. The municipal area includes the cadastral communities of Adnet II, Au, Burgfried, Dürrnberg, Gamp, Gries, Hallein proper, Oberalm II and Taxach. It is linked to the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> by the suburban S-Bahn railway and by the A 10 Tauern Autobahn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area is known for the Hallein Salt Mine on the Dürrnberg plateau, and settlement has been traced back 4,000 years. A Celtic community lived there from 600 BCE until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took over its Noricum kingdom in 15 BCE. In the middle of the 8th century Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tassilo-iii-of-bavaria">Tassilo III of Bavaria</a> granted the estates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> diocese, and from 987 they were held by St Peter's Abbey. Salt became crucial to the wealth of the Archbishopric of Salzburg, competing with the production of Reichenhall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Salt mining is first mentioned in a deed of 1198, and the name Hallein is documented from the early 13th century.</p><p>Beer brewing became common from the 15th century, and in 1489 Prince-Archbishop Leonhard von Keutschach acquired a large brewery whose Hofbräu Kaltenhausen beers became another main source of revenue. At the end of the 17th century Prince-Archbishop Kuenburg had the Protestant miners expelled, and several hundred of them emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walcheren">Walcheren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeelandic-flanders">Zeelandic Flanders</a>. Hallein became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> by 1816. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the town had a work camp annex to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dachau-concentration-camp">Dachau concentration camp</a>, and afterwards a camp for displaced persons, Beth Israel, which closed in 1954.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hallein is a historic town with about 21,150 inhabitants and the second largest in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>. The Keltenmuseum shows the Hallstatt and La Tène Celtic cultures and the development of salt mining through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. The Silent Night Museum documents the life of the composer Franz Xaver Gruber, who lived locally, and holds the guitar used by the lyricist Joseph Mohr.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hallein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Hallstatt itself major activity ended about 500 BCE, when many graves were probably robbed, and salt mining moved to the <b>Hallein</b> mine, whose graves at Dürrnberg run into the early La Tène period, until a great landslide in the mid-4th century BCE destroyed its shafts.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The university was continued, and Salzburg's economy still relied on salt mining at <b>Hallein</b> and trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-salzburg">Electorate of Salzburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The archbishop, elected by the cathedral chapter, governed a land whose wealth came from salt at <b>Hallein</b> and gold and silver mines in the Gastein valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-archbishopric-of-salzburg">Prince-Archbishopric of Salzburg</a></li></ul></section>
